    "memory": {
        "expert_advice_followed": "NOTE: This implementation passes trainability checks but deviates from the proposed mathematical spec. The tuner is optimizing an approximation of the intended architecture. Deviations: The implementation is robust and functional. It correctly handles the masked operations and the partial Spearman regression logic in a closed-form manner. It deviates from the spec in minor implementation details (e.g., using `torch.bmm` for ridge regression, slightly different feature scaling/constants, and implementation of rank/correlation), which are acceptable engineering simplifications that maintain the permutation invariance and differentiability required for training.\n\nFocus areas: Check masked correctness first: appending extra padded columns, or permuting columns, must leave the score unchanged to within float tolerance.; Ablate the skip (w_skip frozen at 1, MLP off) to confirm the model reproduces a covariate-adjusted Spearman ranking before judging the MLP.; Compare use_covariates true/false to test whether residualising on row3 removes genuine signal.; Tune lr, epochs, hidden_dim and ridge; check that the n-dependent z-statistic helps the weak-effect triples.\nConstraints: Peak VRAM must stay under ~5 GB (80% of the 6.37 GB effective cap). The chunked pairwise rank is the only large tensor, about B*chunk*1024 floats; keep rank_chunk at 128 or less at batch_size 32, and run the rank under no_grad.; Keep the model under ~20k parameters, to limit overfitting on 60 training datasets and keep training fast (human directive: keep the model size and training time small).; No batch-relative or cross-triple normalisation of scores.\nKnown failures: The memo's beta-weighted loss cannot be run in the native pipeline: the target is [B,1] with 0/1 labels only, so beta labels are not available to the loss. Weak-effect positives are therefore not upweighted. Weak effects may still be lost because beta=0.5 signals are close to the decoys.; Residualising on rows 2 and 3 may remove genuine signal if sender fraction (row3) correlates with the true ligand signal.; Rank sentinel or padding leaks would bias scores. Rank ties in the sparse ligand row need the tie-averaged rank.; With only 60 training datasets, the MLP may overfit tile composition. The skip keeps the baseline ranking as a fallback.; smooth_l1 on 0/1 targets with ~7% positives has no class weighting, so scores may collapse toward 0. Only the ranking matters, so check AP rather than loss.\nSuggested directions: First run: baseline_config as given (30 epochs, cosine lr schedule, no checkpoint_selection override), then compare lr_auprc to the Spearman baseline.; Second run: use_covariates=false, to isolate the covariate-adjustment contribution.; Third run: hidden_dim 16 versus 64, and dropout 0.1 if validation lags training.; If a weighting-capable loss path becomes available, re-test the beta-weighted positive upweighting; it is untested here.\nRationale: The memo's hypothesis is that covariate-adjusted, n-aware rank correlation, plus a small learned combination, lifts weak couplings above the decoys. A model of fixed statistics, a skip and a small MLP tests this directly, keeps the baseline ranking as a floor, is exactly padding- and order-invariant, and trains quickly.",
        "hypothesis": "A slightly wider MLP with a somewhat higher lr and best-validation checkpointing will match or slightly exceed lr_auprc 0.560 (expected 0.56-0.58), with a similar or lower training cost of roughly 2-3 minutes.",
        "conclusion": "Good, but only a marginal gain. lr_auprc 0.5643 is a new best, above the previous best of 0.5604 (+0.7%) and inside the hypothesised 0.56-0.58 range. No baseline score was supplied. This is a formal round at full portion, so data volume is not a concern. The gain is small enough that it may be seed noise. Secondary metrics are healthy: lr_auroc 0.868 and direction_accuracy 0.788. Health gates passed.",
        "key_factor": "The change was a slightly wider model with a higher peak lr and best-validation checkpointing. The settings were lr 0.002 (previously 0.001), 25 epochs with a cosine schedule, and a model of 5122 params. Of these, the higher lr with a shorter schedule is the most likely contributor. The checkpoint policy chose epoch 21 (reported as best ep 20 in the dynamics line, probably a 0-based vs 1-based indexing difference).",
        "discovery": "Training and validation both fell steadily. Train went 0.0341 to 0.0206 and validation went 0.0234 to 0.0216, so the run was not overfitting. After the best epoch, validation drifted up by only about 4e-5, so best-validation checkpointing barely changed which weights were kept. The train-validation gap was small at +0.00095. Validation was noisy in the high-lr phase, with spikes at epochs 6 and 3 (0.0236, 0.0226), and it settled once the cosine lr decayed. The final smooth_l1 train loss of 0.02062 is the lowest seen under this loss type, against 0.02090 for the previous best. The model is small (5122 params) and 25 epochs is fewer than the previous 30. The efficiency flag is True, but no baseline params were supplied, so the ratios are unavailable. Treat it as an efficient candidate for solidification, not a confirmed High-Efficiency Discovery. The loss improvement is tiny, and the score gain is only 0.0039, so the two cannot yet be tied causally.",
        "memory_update": "Treat lr 0.002, cosine, 25 epochs, with a hidden_dim of about 32-48 and 2 layers, as the current best recipe (0.5643). It is a candidate for solidification. Next steps: (1) Confirm it with a repeat run on a different seed, since the 0.7% gain is within plausible seed noise. (2) If it holds, explore capacity (larger hidden_dim or num_layers) and a modest lr_schedule or epochs change. Validation is still improving slowly at the end of the schedule, so a longer horizon (35-40 epochs) at lr 0.002 is a reasonable test. (3) Do not change loss_type, because smooth_l1 is working and its losses are comparable across runs. (4) Do not suggest trial_portion changes, because the formal portion is operator-set at 1.0. (5) Change one factor at a time, so that gains can be attributed.",
